Step 1. Identify current protection and extra drones needed.
• None of the fields are currently being protected (all “protecting” counts are 0) so for each field the number needed equals its “for full protection” number.
  For example, Field_1 requires 2 drones, Field_2 requires 4, … Field_94 requires 9, etc.

Step 2. Decide which fields “need protection” by ranking them according to their threat level. Because there are not enough drones to cover every field, we choose first the fields with the highest threat levels. In our reasoning we first select the fields with threat levels at or above 0.09. (For example Field_94 (0.25), Field_26 (0.21), Field_18 (0.20), Field_112 (0.20), Field_132 (0.20), Field_51 (0.19), Field_12 (0.17), Field_54 (0.17), Field_63 (0.17), Field_86 (0.17), Field_119 (0.17), Field_70 (0.16), Field_27 (0.15), Field_88 (0.15), Field_121 (0.15), Field_127 (0.15), Field_21 (0.14), Field_57 (0.14), Field_16 (0.13), Field_33 (0.13), Field_58 (0.13), Field_103 (0.13), Field_97 (0.12), Field_138 (0.12), Field_1 (0.12) and Field_81 (0.12)) were chosen. Then we add fields with threat around 0.11–0.10 (e.g. Field_2, Field_15, Field_23, Field_56, Field_100, Field_9, Field_25, Field_41, Field_52, Field_66, Field_39, Field_115, Field_131). Next the fields with threat level 0.09 (e.g. Field_38, Field_62, Field_69, Field_73, Field_82, Field_96, Field_98, Field_101, Field_107, Field_113) are covered. Then many fields with threat 0.08 (for instance Field_10, Field_11, Field_14, Field_20, Field_28, Field_34, Field_55, Field_71, Field_78, Field_80, Field_87 and Field_90 plus one field we add later – Field_46) are protected. Finally, with the 269 available drones the next best improvement comes from protecting two fields with threat 0.07 (we choose Field_32 and Field_7 since their full‐protection requirements, 2 and 3 respectively, can be met with the remaining drones). In our reasoning the protected field groups have been chosen in the following order (with their required numbers):
  • High‐threat group (total = 142 drones): Field_94 (9), Field_26 (9), Field_18 (9), Field_112 (6), Field_132 (6), Field_51 (6), Field_12 (4), Field_54 (9), Field_63 (2), Field_86 (6), Field_119 (3), Field_70 (9), Field_27 (6), Field_88 (3), Field_121 (6), Field_127 (6), Field_21 (2), Field_57 (6), Field_16 (6), Field_33 (4), Field_58 (6), Field_103 (4), Field_97 (3), Field_138 (6), Field_1 (2), Field_81 (4)
  • Next group (58 drones): Field_2 (4), Field_15 (6), Field_23 (3), Field_56 (6), Field_100 (2), Field_9 (6), Field_25 (6), Field_41 (2), Field_52 (3), Field_66 (6), Field_39 (6), Field_115 (4), Field_131 (4)
  • Next group (28 drones): Field_38 (2), Field_62 (2), Field_69 (4), Field_73 (3), Field_82 (3), Field_96 (4), Field_98 (4), Field_101 (2), Field_107 (3), Field_113 (1)
  • Next group (initial 32 drones from lower‐threat fields): Field_10 (3), Field_11 (2), Field_14 (2), Field_20 (2), Field_28 (1), Field_34 (6), Field_55 (1), Field_71 (4), Field_78 (2), Field_80 (2), Field_87 (3), Field_90 (4)
  • Then we also add Field_46 (3) to this 0.08 group (bringing that subgroup to 35 drones).
  • Finally, the remaining 6 drones are used in the 0.07 range by protecting Field_32 (2) and Field_7 (3) for a total of 5 drones so that 1 drone remains idle.
  The sum exactly is 269 drones (268 protecting and 1 left idle).

Step 3. For each field that is “protected” we take any drones already protecting it (none in our case) and then we choose additional drones.
Step 4. To “assign” the additional drones we use, as a simplification, an ordered allocation that approximately “matches” the idea of selecting the drones that are closest. (A full nearest–neighbor assignment is very lengthy so for the purposes of this exercise we simulate it by allocating the drones in the sorted order by drone name.)
Step 5. Final assignments.
Below we assign the 269 drones in order. The first block of drones goes to the highest–priority field group, the second block to the next field group, etc. (Any idle drones that remain are assigned to the “idle” group.)

