Below are the step–by–step results for each field:

Step 1. For each field we “read” the field’s required number of drones (“for full protection”) and note how many drones are already “protecting” it (for example, Field_1 already has two drones protecting it – drones 41 and 139). 
Step 2. We decide that any field with threat level at least 0.10 will be “protected” (even if many drones are already “flying_to_field” for it) while the rest we de–prioritize.
Step 3. For every field that “needs protection” we “keep” drones already protecting it.
Step 4. For each drone that was en route, if its target field is one of the “high-threat” ones then we give it the same “protecting Field_X” group; otherwise we assign it “idle.”
