Step 1. For each field we inspect the current “protecting” count versus the “full‐protection” requirement. For example, Field_1 already has 4 drones protecting and needs 2, so it is “over‐protected,” whereas Field_2 has 0 protecting while needing 4 and Field_6 has 0 protecting while needing 2. Field_9 has 1 protecting while needing 6. (Many other fields are not actively being “attacked” – i.e. they have zero drones “flying to field” – so we choose not to add further protection to them.)

Step 2. We decide that only the fields that are actively in “attack mode” (those with nonzero “flying to field” counts) will be protected. In our situation these active fields are Field_1, Field_2, Field_3, Field_6, Field_9 and Field_140. (Field_1, Field_3 and Field_140 are already “over‐protected” so we leave their protecting drones where they are.) The remaining two, Field_2, Field_6 and Field_9, do not have enough protective coverage.

Step 3. For each active field we “lock in” the drones already in protecting state. For Field_1 (protecting already by Drone_41, Drone_92, Drone_139 and Drone_247), Field_3 (protecting by Drone_117, Drone_127 and Drone_153) and Field_140 (protecting by Drone_72 and Drone_76) no further assignments are needed.

Step 4. Next we select additional drones from those “moving_to_field” and decide which ones are the closest to the field center (the field’s geometric center is the “ideal point” for assignment) so that they are “added” to the protection group:
 • For Field_2 (rectangle from x=31 to 47, y=24 to 39, center roughly [39,31.5]) we need a total of 4 protecting drones. Among the many drones assigned to Field_2 we pick the four closest: Drone_36, Drone_178, Drone_34 and Drone_32.
 • For Field_6 (rectangle from x=284 to 292, y=211 to 227, center roughly [288,219]) we need 2 protecting drones. Among candidates targeting Field_6 the two closest are Drone_61 and Drone_17.
 • For Field_9 (rectangle from x=116 to 139, y=132 to 147, center roughly [127.5,139.5]) we need 5 additional drones (since one is already protecting by Drone_207) so we choose the five closest among the “moving_to_field” candidates: Drone_189, Drone_167, Drone_187, Drone_190 and Drone_166.

Step 5. In the final assignment every drone that is not chosen for one of the “protecting” groups (whether because its target field does not need extra protection or because another drone was closer) is assigned to “idle”. (Drones already “protecting” a field remain in that group.)

Below is the final assignment listing one line for each drone in the format:
  <name>: <group>

Note that if a drone was initially “moving_to_field” or “protecting” a field that is not among our active targets (or if it was not selected as one of the closest needed) it is assigned “idle” in the final solution.

