Below is our step‐by‐step reasoning followed by the final per–drone group assignments.

───────────────────────────── 
Step 1. For every field we check which drones are already “protecting” it (that is, their state is “protecting”) and compare that number to the “for full protection” number for that field. For example, Field_1 already has four drones protecting it (from drones 41, 92, 139 and 247) while it only needs two. Field_2 already has one drone (drone 36) even though four are required. Field_3 already has three protecting drones (drones 117, 127 and 153), exactly the number needed. In many fields no drone is yet protecting the field. (Note that moving drones will in time become protectors but in step 1 we count only those already “protecting”.) 

───────────────────────────── 
Step 2. Because we do not have enough drones to cover every field, we choose to “protect” only those fields that (a) already have some drones assigned – in which case we let them remain so that protection is not lost – or (b) whose “threat level” is high. In our decision we note that while many fields have low threat values (typically below 0.30) we intentionally concentrate our additional protection on the fields in the “high‐threat” group. In our solution we decide (based on the values observed) that the following fields (in addition to those already well covered) will receive extra “idle” drones reassigned from the pool because their threat level is at or above about 0.30:

  • Field_9 (threat level 0.32),
  • Field_15 (0.37),
  • Field_18 (0.35),
  • Field_23 (0.36),
  • Field_25 (0.30),
  • Field_26 (0.42),
  • Field_33 (0.35),
  • Field_54 (0.30),
  • Field_57 (0.35),
  • Field_58 (0.35),
  • Field_66 (0.33),
  • Field_86 (0.38),
  • Field_88 (0.37),
  • Field_94 (0.37),
  • Field_112 (0.39),
  • Field_119 (0.41),
  • Field_132 (0.39).

For any field that already has moving/protecting drones on its way, we assume that extra drones are not needed unless our count shows fewer than full protection. (For example Field_9 already has six drones en route (in “moving_to_field” state) plus one already “protecting”, so no extra idle drone is taken from the pool.) 

───────────────────────────── 
Step 3. For every field that we have decided needs protection, we first “lock‐in” those drones already in “protecting” state. For instance, Field_1, Field_3 and Field_140 already have enough (“protecting”) drones–even if their threat is low we let them remain as is. 

───────────────────────────── 
Step 4. Then for every field that needs protection but does not already have enough “in‐place” or “moving” drones we assign one (or more) of the idle drones that are closest (by approximate location) to the field’s rectangle. In our solution we have chosen one idle drone per field among the high–threat fields as follows:

  • For Field_15 (rectangle 13–36,186–201, threat 0.37) we choose drone 7 (at 30,187).  
  • For Field_18 (259–282,16–39, threat 0.35) we choose drone 104 (at 268,25).  
  • For Field_23 (164–187,226–233, threat 0.36) we choose drone 46 (at 184,231).  
  • For Field_25 (35–59,144–160, threat 0.30) we choose drone 40 (at 59,142).  
  • For Field_26 (270–293,156–180, threat 0.42) we choose drone 16 (at 277,152).  
  • For Field_33 (174–190,54–69, threat 0.35) we choose drone 182 (at 179,54).  
  • For Field_54 (233–257,52–75, threat 0.30) we choose drone 15 (at 229,52).  
  • For Field_57 (121–144,278–293, threat 0.35) we choose drone 110 (at 142,279).  
  • For Field_58 (126–142,185–208, threat 0.35) we choose drone 101 (at 108,184) which is close enough.  
  • For Field_66 (197–220,152–168, threat 0.33) we choose drone 132 (at 201,158).  
  • For Field_86 (109–132,78–93, threat 0.38) we choose drone 82 (at 93,112).  
  • For Field_88 (211–218,123–146, threat 0.37) we choose drone 9 (at 229,116).  
  • For Field_94 (60–84,58–81, threat 0.37) we choose drone 88 (at 58,82).  
  • For Field_112 (95–111,184–207, threat 0.39) we choose drone 154 (at 100,181).  
  • For Field_119 (179–187,77–101, threat 0.41) we choose drone 109 (at 199,83).  
  • For Field_132 (190–205,269–293, threat 0.39) we choose drone 55 (at 190,271).  
  • Also note that Field_6 (which needs a total of 2 and already has one protecting plus one moving) gets a final extra assignment from an idle drone – we choose drone 124 (at 240,226) for Field_6.

───────────────────────────── 
Step 5. We now give the final group assignment. (Any drone already “moving_to_field” or “protecting” keeps its original group unless we have reassigned an idle drone for additional support. All other idle drones not assigned remain “idle”.)

