Below is the complete “step‐by‐step” reasoning that I followed. (Note that in a “real‐world” system many factors – distance, overall drone availability relative to every field’s “flying” count, and the relative threat – must be “balanced” by an optimization routine. In the simplified solution below we have used a “threshold‐by‐threat” rule: only fields whose “threat level” is at least 0.10 will be (actively) defended, while fields with 0.00 or “marginal” (below 0.10) threat are not considered worth additional protection. Also, any drone already in “protecting” state for a field that does not “deserve” protection based on its threat gets re–assigned idle. In addition, if a drone is “moving to” a field that is not in the set of fields to protect, the drone is “re–assigned” idle. Finally, if a drone is already idle then it stays idle.)

Step 1. For each field we checked its “full protection requirement” versus the “threat level” and noted that even though many fields have several drones already “flying” (or in “protecting” state) the natural decision here is to spend our available drone assets on the “most threatened” fields. (For example, Field_2 has a 0.25 threat level – well above the threshold – whereas Field_1 (0.00) or Field_3 (0.00) does not deserve any extra protection.) 

Step 2. Based on a threshold of 0.10 we decided that a field “needs protection” only if its (current) threat level is at least 0.10. In our simplified decision we then do the following:
 – If a drone’s target field is among those with threat level ≥ 0.10 then that drone is assigned into the “protecting Field_X” group for that field.
 – Otherwise (if the target field has threat level < 0.10) the drone is re–assigned “idle”.
 – Also, any drone already in “protecting” state that is ‘defending’ a field whose threat is below 0.10 is “re–assigned” idle.

Step 3. Then we “lock–in” the assignment for each field by keeping the drones that already are protecting it (if their field’s threat is high) and adding in those that are “moving‐to” that field if its threat level is high. (For example, Field_2 has threat 0.25 so any drone with target Field_2 is now assigned “protecting Field_2”. In contrast Field_140 has threat 0.00 so all drones with that target become idle.)

Step 4. Finally the decision is applied drone–by–drone. (When inspecting every drone we look up its “target field” (if any) and then compare the field’s threat level. For our final answer we use the allowed protective groups exactly as given. For instance, if a drone’s target is “Field_4” (with threat 0.10) then we output “protecting Field_4”. If a drone’s target is, for example, “Field_1” (threat 0.00) or “Field_3” (0.00) or “Field_140” (0.00) then we assign that drone “idle”. Also drones originally “idle” remain “idle”.)

Step 5. Here is the final drone–by–drone assignment (one line per Drone in the required format). Note that only the drones whose target field is among those with threat level ≥0.10 (Field_2, Field_4, Field_5, Field_9, … etc) get “protecting …” assignments; every other drone is set to “idle.”

