Step 1. Identify for each field the “protection‐deficit” given the field’s required number of drones versus those already protecting it. For example, Field_2 (threat level 0.24) requires 4 drones and already has three drones protecting it (as seen in the sensors’ field info and confirmed by our drone list – note that in our drone list there are already three drones with group “protecting Field_2” from past assignments: Drone_36, Drone_103 and Drone_178). Thus Field_2 needs one extra drone. In Field_4 the sensor report says 8 drones are already protecting it though the requirement is only 4; so it is “over‐protected” and no extra drones are needed. Field_5 (threat = 0.13) has no protecting drones even though it needs 2; so it must receive exactly 2 protecting drones. Field_9 (threat = 0.05) already has eight protecting drones even though it requires 6; hence no extra assignment is necessary. (All other fields either have threat level 0 or are lower‐priority so we decide not to assign any drones to them.) 

Step 2. Decide which fields need protection. Because our goal is to minimize damage and we are limited in drone resources, we choose to assign protection only to the most threatened fields. In our “high‐priority” list we choose fields whose threat level is nonzero and whose current protection is insufficient. In our simplified decision we select Field_2 (needs one extra drone) and Field_5 (needs two drones). Although Field_4 and Field_9 already have protecting drones (even an over‐protection in Field_4), we keep those drones already in place; all drones that were “en‐route” (moving_to_field) to Field_2, Field_4 or Field_5 that are not needed will be released (assigned “idle”). (Any drone targeted to a field that is not among the “priority” ones or is already fully protected we assign to idle.) 

Step 3. For each field that needs protection we keep the drones already “protecting” it. For Field_2 we already have Drone_36, Drone_103, and Drone_178 protecting it. For Field_4 we keep the protecting drones: Drone_8, Drone_13, Drone_15, Drone_104, Drone_107, Drone_144 and Drone_269. For Field_9 the protecting drones remain: Drone_166, Drone_167, Drone_169, Drone_171, Drone_187, Drone_189, Drone_190 and Drone_207.

Step 4. Now we choose the “additional” drones from those that are moving toward the field and that are closest to the field. For Field_2 the best candidate is Drone_34 (its location of 52,29 is very near to Field_2’s rectangle (left=31, top=24, right=47, bottom=39)). Thus we designate Drone_34 to protect Field_2. For Field_5 we must choose 2 drones from among those headed toward Field_5. Examining the list we see that the two drones whose positions are closest to Field_5’s rectangle (left=248, top=226, right=256, bottom=242) are Drone_208 (at 239,239) and Drone_237 (at 243,217). They are therefore selected to be assigned to “protecting Field_5.”

Step 5. Finally, every drone that is not needed for the above “protecting” decisions is assigned to the “idle” group. (That means every drone that was originally “moving_to_field” but whose target was not among our selected assignments or that exceeded the protection need for that field is set to “idle.” Also, drones already “idle” remain idle.)

