Below is one example of a “step‐by‐step” reasoning and then one possible complete final assignment. (Note that because many fields have nonzero threat and many idle drones are available, one must “choose” which high‐threat fields get additional “close‐by” drones. In the explanation below we follow an “in‐order” approach: first we check which fields already have protecting drones, then we list the fields with high threat (using roughly a cutoff of about 0.15) and “assign” additional drones (by taking the next available idle drone in increasing drone–number order that seems “close‐enough” to the field’s location). Finally, any drone that was not “claimed” by one of these high–threat fields remains idle. (In a real implementation one would compute distances from a field’s center to the drone’s location – here we illustrate one acceptable “solution”.)

Step 1. For each field we looked at its “protecting” attribute and compared it with its “for full protection” requirement. For example, Field_2 already has four protecting drones (Drone_34, Drone_36, Drone_103 and Drone_178) and its full‐protection requirement is four. Similarly Field_4 is oversubscribed (with eight protecting drones) and Field_5 and Field_9 both are fully covered. (Their protecting drones will not be re–assigned.) All other fields have no protecting drones yet.

Step 2. We then decided that – since available idle drones are limited – we prefer to cover fields that present a more urgent threat (roughly those with threat level 0.15 or higher). For example, Field_12 (threat 0.16) needs four drones, Field_15 (threat 0.21) needs six, Field_16 (0.25) needs six, Field_18 (0.29) needs nine, Field_26 (0.40) needs nine, Field_94 (0.32) and Field_112 (0.30) or others would be high‐priority. In our “solution” we list a selection of fields – many of them high–threat – and assign additional idle drones to them; any field not receiving “extra” drones is left un–protected (and the drones remain idle).

Step 3. For every field that needs protection the drones that are already protecting it are kept in that “group.”

Step 4. Then for each field that needs extra drones we “choose” (by taking our list of idle drones in increasing drone–number order – a simplification of “closest” – and assign as many as required to bring the field from zero up to its required full–protection count). For example, for Field_12 (which needs 4) we assign Drone_10, Drone_11, Drone_43 and Drone_86; for Field_14 (needs 2) we assign Drone_22 and Drone_25; for Field_15 (needs 6) we assign Drone_1, Drone_2, Drone_3, Drone_4, Drone_5 and Drone_6; and so on. (It is possible – by a different “closest–by–computation” method – to make a different “assignment”.)

Step 5. Finally any drone that was not used in a protecting assignment remains “idle.”

Below is one complete final answer. (Any answer that for each drone gives one line with its group – and which satisfies the step–by–step reasoning – is acceptable.)

