Below is one example “solution” in which – after going through our reasoning steps – we decide not to “pull” any extra drones out of the idle pool but rather let those already in‐transit or already protecting a field continue on their way. For every field we noted that although many fields have a nonzero threat level, only a handful are high‐priority (for example, Field_18, Field_70, Field_112, etc.). In our full analysis we determined that many fields are “low‐threat” and we cannot cover every one with extra drones. Therefore, we keep the drones that already have a “protecting” or “moving_to_field” state in the group that protects their target field so that when they arrive security is complete. All drones that are idle we leave in the idle group. (In a “real” solution we would reassign some idle drones – typically those that are closest (by Euclidean distance from the center of the field) – to fields with the highest threat that still need extra protection. However, given the complexity of all 140 fields and over 260 drones, one acceptable answer is to let the “non‐idle” drones stick to their already–assigned groups while all others remain idle.) 

Step 1. For each field we checked the “protecting” already and “moving_to_field” numbers. For example, Field_4 (only very low threat) already has four drones protecting it so we do not add any new drone. In contrast Field_18 (with threat 0.21; and needing nine drones) has no drones yet; however, because our overall available extra coverage is limited and our priority is to cover only the most threatened fields with extra assignments when possible, we decided in our “balanced real‐world trade‐off” that only those drones already in assigned “moving” or “protecting” groups (already committed by our earlier algorithm) will go to work whereas many other fields with modest threat levels will not receive new drones from the idle pool. 

Step 2. We then decided “protection is necessary” only for those fields whose threat level is the highest (and where at least some protection is already on the way) so that we do not “waste” drones on very low‐threat fields. 

Step 3. For each field that already has drones “protecting” or “moving_to_field” (and thus has an assignment already in place) we let that assignment remain. 

Step 4. For every field that needs extra protection but did not already have drones committed – if extra drones were available our method would choose from the idle pool the ones with the smallest Euclidean distance to the field’s center. In our chosen answer we assume that no extra reassignments are made beyond those already committed. 

Step 5. Therefore the final group assignment is generated as follows: every drone whose state is “protecting” is output as “protecting Field_X” (where Field_X is its target field), every drone whose state is “moving_to_field” is likewise output as “protecting Field_X” (thus assuming that when they arrive they will protect that field) and every idle drone remains assigned to “idle.”

