Step 1. Identify for each “active” field those drones that are already in “protecting” state (as reported both by the field’s “protecting” attribute and from the drone list) and compute how many more are needed:
• Field_1 (full protection=2, already protecting=0) needs 2 extra drones.
• Field_2 (full protection=4, already protecting=2 – namely drones that were “protecting Field_2” such as Drone_209 and Drone_210) needs 2 more.
• Field_3 (full protection=3, already protecting=1 – e.g. Drone_141) needs 2 more.
• Field_4 (full protection=4, already protecting=0) needs 4.
• Field_5 (full protection=2, already protecting=2 such as Drone_14 and Drone_216) needs 0.
• Field_6 (full protection=2 but threat level is 0.00) will not be given extra protection.
• Field_7 (full protection=3 but its threat level is 0.00) will not be “protected” further (drones already “protecting” it will be re‐assigned to idle).
• Field_8 (full protection=2, already protecting=1 – Drone_9) needs 1 extra.
• Field_9 (full protection=6, already protecting=8 such as Drone_63, Drone_115, Drone_116, Drone_145, Drone_167, Drone_169 and Drone_207) is well protected.
• Field_140 (full protection=2, already protecting=0) needs 2 extra.

Step 2. Decide which fields truly “need protection” (i.e. which have nonzero threat and are not already “over‐protected”):
The highest–threat fields (by threat level and current shortfall) are chosen. In our list the fields with nonzero threat that would benefit are:
 Field_140 (threat 0.07, needing 2), Field_2 (threat 0.04, needing 2), Field_1 (threat 0.03, needing 2), Field_4 (threat 0.03, needing 4), Field_8 (threat 0.02, needing 1) and Field_3 (threat 0.02, needing 2). Fields with zero threat or already having more than required (Field_5, Field_7, Field_9, Field_6) will not receive extra drone protection (their drones will be re‑assigned to idle).

Step 3. For those fields chosen to be protected, we “lock in” the drones that are already protecting:
• Field_1: none yet.
• Field_2: keep Drone_209 and Drone_210.
• Field_3: keep Drone_141.
• Field_4: none are “protecting” so far.
• Field_8: keep Drone_9.
• Field_140: none are “protecting.”

Step 4. Now “fill out” the additional positions (using the drones that are moving toward each target and are the closest to the fields’ centers) as follows:
• Field_1 (center roughly [213,77]) needs 2 extra. Among drones with target Field_1, the two closest (by our approximate distance calculations) are Drone_88 (≈45 units away) and Drone_89 (≈59 units). So we assign Drone_88 and Drone_89 to protecting Field_1.
• Field_2 (center about [39,31.5]) needs 2 extra. Among those moving to Field_2 the very closest is Drone_178 (located at [44,28], only about 6 units away) and then Drone_246 (at [48,41], roughly 13 units away). So we assign Drone_178 and Drone_246 to protecting Field_2.
• Field_3 (center roughly [23,71]) needs 2 extra. Among those with target Field_3 the closest are Drone_175 (at [34,85], ~18 units away) and Drone_176 (at [37,78], ~16 units away). So we assign Drone_175 and Drone_176 to protecting Field_3.
• Field_4 (center roughly [237.5,36]) needs 4. Among drones moving to Field_4 the four closest (by approximate distance) turn out to be Drone_41 (around 80 units away), Drone_45 (also ~80 units), then Drone_43 (~103 units) and Drone_46 (~116 units). So we assign Drone_41, Drone_45, Drone_43, and Drone_46 to protecting Field_4.
• Field_8 (center roughly [264.5,91.5]) needs 1 extra. Among the many with target Field_8 the closest is Drone_5 (located at [243,87], a difference of roughly 21–22 units), so we assign Drone_5 to protecting Field_8.
• Field_140 (center roughly [162.5,176.5]) needs 2. Among those with target Field_140 the closest are Drone_20 (at [168,181], almost 7 units away) and Drone_25 (at [204,179] is about 41 units away, which is preferable to the others farther away). So we assign Drone_20 and Drone_25 to protecting Field_140.

Step 5. All drones that are not assigned by the above steps should be put in the “idle” group. (Also note that if any drone was already “protecting” a field that we do not very much need to protect – such as Field_7 – we re‑assign that drone as idle.)

