Step 1. For every field we first note which drones are already “protecting” that field (by checking each drone’s current state and target field) and compare that number with the “for full protection” count listed in the field‐data. For example, Field_3 needs 3 drones for full protection but among its drones (Drone_4, Drone_5, Drone_29, Drone_30, Drone_31, Drone_113, Drone_114, Drone_146, Drone_147, Drone_148, Drone_198, Drone_199, Drone_200) only those that are explicitly “protecting” (or will be assigned to protect when we decide the field needs active protection) will count. (Many drones that are “moving_to_field” will be “promoted” to protecting when we decide that field deserves protection.)  
 
Step 2. Then we review each field’s threat level. In our plan we decide that a field with a threat level of 0.00 (or very low, for example 0.01) is not a priority for protection so even if drones are already heading there we “cancel” that protect assignment and instead send those drones “idle”. On the other hand, fields with moderate–to–high threat levels (for example 0.03 up to 0.15) are prioritized. For instance, Field_2 (threat 0.03), Field_3 (0.03), Field_4 (0.03), Field_6 (0.03), Field_10 (0.08), Field_13 (0.07), Field_15 (0.08), Field_16 (0.10), Field_18 (0.14) etc. will get protection. In our final simplified decision we decide that if a field’s threat level is 0.00 or very low (like 0.01 or 0.02 in many cases) we do not expend additional resources – we “cancel” such protecting efforts. (Note that in many cases the “for full protection” number is defined even if threat level is 0.00; here we choose not to protect if the threat is essentially zero.)  
 
Step 3. For every field needing protection we “lock‐in” the protecting drones (if any) that are already in the protecting state. For all drones that are “moving_to_field” for a field that we decide to protect we reassign them to the appropriate “protecting Field_x” group.  
 
Step 4. For every field we assign drones based on their current “target field” status if that field is one we wish to protect. Otherwise, all drones with that target (or idle with no target) are reassigned to idle. We use a “closest–first” rule but here (for illustration) we simply use each drone’s original targeted field as a signal. If its target is a field we wish to protect, its final assignment is “protecting Field_x”. If not, it is reassigned to idle.
 
Step 5. Finally we produce one line per drone “Drone_x: group” where group is one of the allowed groups. In our final grouping we decide as follows:
 
• Any drone whose “target field” (or protecting state) is for a field that by our decision does not need extra protection (because its threat level is 0.00 or very low) is sent to idle.
• Any drone whose target is for a field that is above threshold (for this example we treat threat levels 0.03 and above as worth “protecting”) is assigned to “protecting Field_x”.
 
Below is our final assignment. (Notice that for example Field_1, Field_5, Field_7, Field_8, Field_9, Field_11, Field_12, …, Field_21, etc. are not actually “protected” because their threat is very low; so every drone that was originally heading for one of those fields is reassigned idle.) 

