Step 1: For each field we first inspect the current “protecting” and “moving_to_field” assignments (which we count as already‐committed to that field) versus the “for full protection” requirement. For example, Field_6 requires 2 protecting drones but already has four drones in “protecting” plus many more en‐route (“moving_to_field”). Similarly Field_4 is already protected by four drones, and Field_2 already has three “protecting” drones plus many “moving_to_field” ones so that its full quota (of 4) is well exceeded. In contrast, many fields with nonzero threat levels have not yet been “targeted” by any drone. For example, Field_1 (threat level 0.04, full requirement 2) has no protecting or moving‐to assignment, so we must assign two additional drones that are closest to its center (approximately at x≈213 and y≈77). Likewise Field_8 (threat level 0.01, full requirement 2), and Field_9 (threat level 0.02, full protection requires 6 drones) have no “protecting” drones yet. In these cases we choose among the idle drones those whose locations are nearest the field’s geometric center.

Step 2: Because our overall pool of 269 drones is limited but many fields show some threat (threat level greater than zero) we decide to “protect” only those fields that are in danger – that is, those with a nonzero threat level whose fields are not already (vastly) covered by incoming protection. (For many fields the streams of “moving_to_field” assignments already more than exceed the full‐protection count; we leave those assignments unchanged.) In our example the highest‐threat fields (for example, Field_18, Field_25, Field_26, Field_56, Field_57, Field_62, Field_70, Field_132, etc.) are already receiving ample protection either by virtue of their high number of “moving_to_field” drones or – when necessary – will be bolstered by an extra assignment from our idle pool. In this solution we illustrate the process by “topping‐up” protection on a few fields that currently have no drones assigned at all (namely Field_1, Field_8, Field_9, and Field_10) while leaving the other fields’ assignments unchanged.

Step 3: For every field that already has drones “protecting” (or flying toward) it in our current list, we let those assignments remain. For example, every drone whose state is “moving_to_field” or “protecting” remains in the group “protecting Field_X” where X is its target field.

Step 4: For every field that requires extra drones we choose from the idle pool those that are closest. In our solution we “top‐up”:
 • Field_1 (center approximately (213,77)) needs 2 drones. We assign Drone_8 and Drone_21 (both idle and close by) to “protecting Field_1”.
 • Field_8 (center approximately (264.5,92)) needs 2 drones. We assign Drone_9 and Drone_102.
 • Field_9 (center approximately (127.5,140)) needs 6 drones. We assign Drone_54, Drone_67, Drone_68, Drone_69, Drone_126 and Drone_142.
 • Field_10 (center approximately (72,229)) needs 3 drones. Even though this field is a bit far‐flung from most already idle drones, we choose those that are “closest” in our list – here we assign Drone_228, Drone_261 and Drone_268.
(All other idle drones – and those already committed by being “moving_to_field” or “protecting” – remain in their current group.)

Step 5: In our final answer we list one line per drone. For a drone whose state is “moving_to_field” or “protecting” we output the group “protecting Field_X” (with X the target field). For an idle drone that was not re‐assigned to help top‐up a field’s protection, we output “idle”. (Note that only the few idle drones we mentioned are re‐assigned to protection; all others stay idle.) 

Below is the final assignment. (Any drone that was already flying toward a field or protecting that field is left unchanged.)

