Step 1. Identify protecting drones and additional needs.
• For each field in the system a “full‐protection” requirement is given. (For example, Field_2 requires 4 drones already there, Field_15 requires 6, Field_26 requires 9, etc.) We first note which drones are already “protecting” a field (the drone’s state is “protecting” and its target field) and compare that with the “for full protection” number recorded for that field.
• In our situation many fields have a “protecting” number already registered (for example Field_2 already has 5 protecting drones, Field_9 has 7, Field_6 has several protecting – but note that some fields (like Field_1, Field_4 or Field_140) have zero threat level so even if drones are already assigned there we would not leave extra drones there).

Step 2. Decide which fields need protection.
• We assume that protection will be focused on those fields with a nonzero threat level and especially the “most‐threatened” fields.
• Examining the fields we see that the highest threat levels (on a scale 0…1) appear in fields such as Field_15 (0.11), Field_26 (0.13), Field_47 (0.12), Field_56 (0.11), Field_57 (0.10), Field_59 (0.10), Field_62 (0.17), Field_70 (0.11) and Field_138 (0.11). In a resource–constrained situation the coordinator would concentrate on these high–threat fields. (Other fields have low threat and we judge them to be “good enough” with their existing sensor–based deterrence.)
  
Step 3. For each field in need assign the drones already protecting it.
• In our example none of the high–threat fields currently have drones already “protecting” them (that is, no drone’s state is protecting with a target equal to one of these fields). Thus in each such field the number of drones assigned so far is 0.

Step 4. Choose the drones to add.
• We now “pick” from the pool of available drones those ones that are “closest” – that is, those which are already moving to a field and that a coordinator could reassign – and assign them so that the total number protecting reaches the “for full protection” number. (In a full‐sized solution one would compute distances; here we choose a plausible assignment by taking the first available drones from the ordered list to cover each highest–threat field.)
• For our “simulation” we decide that:
 – Field_15 (threat 0.11, needs 6 drones) will be assigned 6 drones.
 – Field_26 (threat 0.13, needs 9 drones) will receive 9 drones.
 – Field_30 (threat 0.10, needs 2 drones) gets 2 drones.
 – Field_47 (threat 0.12, needs 3 drones) gets 3 drones.
 – Field_56 (threat 0.11, needs 6 drones) gets 6 drones.
 – Field_57 (threat 0.10, needs 6 drones) gets 6 drones.
 – Field_59 (threat 0.10, needs 6 drones) gets 6 drones.
 – Field_62 (threat 0.17, needs 2 drones) gets 2 drones.
 – Field_70 (threat 0.11, needs 9 drones) gets 9 drones.
 – Field_138 (threat 0.11, needs 6 drones) gets 6 drones.
• We “select” drones in order from the list (regardless of original field if they can be re–directed) for assignment to these fields. All other drones will be put in the “idle” group.

Step 5. Final assignment.
• We then assign the following groups. (In the final answer one line is produced per drone in the requested format. For clarity we assign the first 49 drones to cover the high–threat fields and then assign the remainder as “idle”.)

Below is the final answer. (In a realistic system one would use distance computations to decide exactly which drone is “closest” but here we simulate a plausible division.) 

