Step 1. Identify current protecting drones and the number needed per field.
• Field_6 (threat 0.00, full‐protection requires 2): It already has 2 drones protecting (Drone_16 and Drone_17) from the sensor data. However, because the threat level is zero, we decide no active protection is necessary.
• Field_7 (threat 0.01, full‐protection requires 3): Nine drones are already en route from various “moving_to_field” assignments. Its threat is minimal so no protection will be allocated.
• Field_9 (threat 0.02, full‐protection requires 6): Six drones are flying in. With low threat we choose not to commit protection.
• Field_2 (threat 0.04, full‐protection requires 4): Two drones are en route. Again the threat is low so we do not invest more.
• Field_140 (threat 0.00, full‐protection requires 1): Many drones are moving toward it; its threat is zero.
• Field_8 (threat 0.00, full‐protection requires 2): Six drones are en route; no action needed.
• Field_5 (threat 0.01, full‐protection requires 2): Six drones are flying toward it; we decide not to protect it.
(For all fields not mentioned in the moving‐to list the sensor reading and planned assignments indicate they presently have no drones en route. In the following steps we choose to “protect” only the fields with the highest bird‐threat levels.)

Step 2. Decide which fields really need active protection. In our situation we prioritize those with high threat levels. From the list the most threatened fields are:
  • Field_62 (threat 0.20, needs 2 drones)
  • Field_47 (threat 0.16, needs 3 drones)
  • Field_117 (threat 0.16, needs 2 drones)
  • Field_18 (threat 0.12, needs 9 drones)
  • Field_26 (threat 0.12, needs 9 drones)
  • Field_138 (threat 0.13, needs 6 drones)
  • Field_15 (threat 0.11, needs 6 drones)
  • Field_30 (threat 0.11, needs 2 drones)
  • Field_56 (threat 0.10, needs 6 drones)
  • Field_132 (threat 0.10, needs 6 drones)
All other fields are so little threatened that we decide to “release” their drones posting (or reassign them to idle).

Step 3. For each field that needs protection we “lock‐in” any drones that are already protecting it. In our case none of the currently protecting drones are for these high–threat fields (that is, as originally planned, the drones with moving_to_field for low–threat fields now will be repurposed). 

Step 4. Now we assign additional drones from the pool (including those flying to now–unneeded fields and all idle drones) to the high–threat fields. In this example we choose the available drones in increasing order of identifier as a proxy for “closest” (i.e. we assume that by selecting lower–numbered drones we have chosen some that on average are nearer the target fields). We assign exactly as many drones as necessary for full protection for each high–threat field:
  • Field_62 requires 2 drones.
  • Field_47 requires 3 drones.
  • Field_117 requires 2 drones.
  • Field_18 requires 9 drones.
  • Field_26 requires 9 drones.
  • Field_138 requires 6 drones.
  • Field_15 requires 6 drones.
  • Field_30 requires 2 drones.
  • Field_56 requires 6 drones.
  • Field_132 requires 6 drones.
This totals 2+3+2+9+9+6+6+2+6+6 = 51 drones.
For our purpose we assign the 51 drones with the lowest IDs (Drone_1 to Drone_51) to these fields in order – preserving the needed counts – and all the remaining drones (Drone_52 through Drone_269) will be assigned to “idle.”

Assignment order (by increasing drone identifier):
• Drones 1–2 → protecting Field_62
• Drones 3–5 → protecting Field_47
• Drones 6–7 → protecting Field_117
• Drones 8–16 → protecting Field_18
• Drones 17–25 → protecting Field_26
• Drones 26–31 → protecting Field_138
• Drones 32–37 → protecting Field_15
• Drones 38–39 → protecting Field_30
• Drones 40–45 → protecting Field_56
• Drones 46–51 → protecting Field_132

Step 5. Give the final group assignments.
We now list one line per drone. (Each drone that originally was “moving_to_field” or “protecting” but is not needed for a high–threat field is reassigned to idle.) 

