Step 1. Identify already‐protecting drones and extra drones needed.
• Field_2 (threat 0.03, full protection requires 4 drones): No drone is “protecting” even though eight drones are currently “moving_to_field Field_2.” Thus it already has a flying complement well in excess of the “4 needed” but because its threat is very low we eventually will not protect it.
• Field_7 (threat 0.01, full req. 3): Five drones are flying to Field_7 and none protecting yet. Still, its threat level is very low so we will not commit resources here.
• Field_8 (threat 0.00, full req. 2): Thirteen drones are en‐route. Its threat level is nil so no protection is needed.
• Field_6 (threat 0.00, full req. 2): One drone is “protecting” and 13 more are flying to Field_6. Its threat level is zero so we will not assign additional protection.
• Field_5 (threat 0.01, req. 2): Six drones are en‐route. Again, threat is low.
• Field_140 (threat 0.02, req. 1): Two drones are flying toward Field_140.
In summary the moving groups in our state are all aimed at fields that have very low threat levels. We now decide in step 2 that we want to assign protection only to the fields with high bird‐threat levels. (Since our overall objective is to minimize damage – and we cannot fully protect every field – we prefer to use available resources on the most threatened fields.)

Step 2. Decide which fields need protection.
Among the many fields described, we choose—for the purpose of this assignment—the highest‐threat fields. For example, we select the following fields to be fully protected (we imagine these to be “high‐priority” because their threat level is above roughly 0.07):
  • Field_15 (threat 0.11, full req. 6)
  • Field_47 (threat 0.17, req. 3)
  • Field_18 (threat 0.15, req. 9)
  • Field_26 (threat 0.12, req. 9)
  • Field_56 (threat 0.12, req. 6)
  • Field_70 (threat 0.10, req. 9)
  • Field_112 (threat 0.11, req. 6)
  • Field_117 (threat 0.11, req. 2)
  • Field_132 (threat 0.10, req. 6)
  • Field_94 (threat 0.08, req. 9)
  • Field_97 (threat 0.08, req. 3)
  • Field_138 (threat 0.09, req. 6)
  • Field_59 (threat 0.09, req. 6)
  • Field_114 (threat 0.07, req. 2)
All other fields (including those that already have drones flying to them but that have low threat levels) will get no additional protection and the drones initially “moving_to_field” for those become idle.

Step 3. For each field needing protection we “lock‐in” any drones already protecting it. (In our case no drone is actively protecting any of the high‐priority fields.)
 
Step 4. For each field needing protection we assign the “closest” (that is, we choose from among our idle pool in order of increasing drone number) idle drones from the overall pool. (The drones already “moving_to_field” to low‑threat fields are not reassigned; for the purpose of our answer we reassign them to “idle.”)
We decide therefore to reassign every drone that originally was “moving_to_field” to one of the non–high‑threat fields to “idle.” Then we reassign a subset of the idle drones (starting with the idle drone having the smallest number) to protect the following high‑threat fields:
  • Field_15: needs 6 drones → assign idle drones Drone_52 through Drone_57.
  • Field_47: needs 3 drones → assign next idle drones Drone_58 – Drone_60.
  • Field_18: needs 9 drones → assign Drone_61 through Drone_69.
  • Field_26: needs 9 drones → assign Drone_70 through Drone_78.
  • Field_56: needs 6 drones → assign Drone_79 through Drone_84.
  • Field_70: needs 9 drones → assign Drone_85 through Drone_93.
  • Field_112: needs 6 drones → assign Drone_94 through Drone_99.
  • Field_117: needs 2 drones → assign Drone_100 and Drone_101.
  • Field_132: needs 6 drones → assign Drone_102 through Drone_107.
  • Field_94: needs 9 drones → assign Drone_108 through Drone_116.
  • Field_97: needs 3 drones → assign Drone_117 through Drone_119.
  • Field_138: needs 6 drones → assign Drone_120 through Drone_125.
  • Field_59: needs 6 drones → assign Drone_126 through Drone_131.
  • Field_114: needs 2 drones → assign Drone_132 and Drone_133.
All the remaining drones (including those originally “moving_to_field” for low‑priority fields and all idle drones from Drone_134 onward) will be “idle.”

