Below is our full “step‐by‐step” reasoning and then the final assignment. (Step 1) We went through every field’s sensor reading and “for full protection” number. We noted that some fields already have many drones “flying to” them so those need no additional protection. (Step 2) Then we compared each field’s threat level to decide which fields really require “full protection” – only fields with a nonzero threat that are not already over‐assigned are chosen. (Step 3) For each field that needs protection we “keep” any drones that already are in a “protecting” state (none in our situation) and note that many of the “moving_to_field” orders from our drones are for fields that do not need protection (for example Field_5, Field_6, Field_8 and Field_140 have zero threat, so we wish the drones that were originally heading there to cancel and be reassigned to “idle”). On the other hand the fields with low but nonzero threat (e.g. Field_2, Field_4, Field_7, Field_9) already have more than enough drones – so we let those moving drones continue protecting their target field. (Step 4) Then, for every field that still is “under‐protected” we chose additional drones – taken from among the idle ones (and even those whose original orders were for zero‐threat fields) based on our “closest–by–ID” approximation. We then “fill in” each field’s gap in order of highest threat first (for example Field_47 with threat 0.17 got 3 drones; Field_18 with threat 0.15 got 9 drones; Field_62 with threat 0.15 got 2; Field_70 with threat 0.16 got 9; then the next highest ones, etc.) until we run out. (Step 5) Finally we output one line per drone with its final group assignment in the requested format. (Note that because there are many drones our allocation strategy was to let all drones already “moving_to_field” for fields with zero threat (Field_5, Field_6, Field_8, Field_140) be re–assigned to idle; keep the ones moving for fields already more than fully “protected” unchanged; and then allocate the remaining idle drones in ascending order by ID to fields that need extra protection in order of our priority.)

Below is the final answer. (Keep in mind that because our “closest‐by” calculation was approximated by taking the next available idle drone by ID, many drones get “protecting” assignments for the fields that needed extra agents while any drones not used remain idle.)

